|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_polar_conversion_

    Show that for all $A, B \in \rr$ there exists
    $R_0$, $\theta_0 \in \rr$ such that

    $$
    A\cos x + B\sin x = R_0\cdot\cos(x - \theta_0)
    $$

    for all $x \in \rr$.

    |> Solution

        Starting from the desired endpoint, note that

        $$
        \cos(x - \theta_0) = \cos x\cos\theta_0 + \sin x \sin \theta_0
        $$

        by the “angle-difference formula” (cf. Exercise >>_unit_dot_product_geometric_interpretation_) so

        $$
        R_0\cdot\cos(x - \theta_0)
        $$

        equals

        $$
        (R_0\cos\theta_0)\cdot\cos x + (R_0\sin\theta_0)\cdot\sin x
        $$

        where we suggestively group the terms to underscore
        that all we need to do in order to “win” is to satisfy 
        the equations 

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        A &=         R_0\cos\theta_0,\\
        B &= \up{1.5}R_0\rt{0.1}\sin\theta_0\,
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        and this can be done simply by setting $(R_0, \theta_0)$ to
        be the polar coordinates of the cartesian point $(A, B) \in \rr^2$!
        I.e., put

        $$
        R_0 = \sqrt{A^2 + B^2}
        $$

        and...

        $$
        \theta_0 = \begin{cases}
        \arctan(B/A)&\te{if }\, A \ne 0, \,\te{else} \\
        \up{1.2}\te{arccot}(A/B) &\te{if }\, B \ne 0, \,\te{else} \\
        \up{1.2}\rm{anything} &\te{if }\, A = 0, B = 0
        \end{cases}
        $$

        ...where we copy-paste a formula from the chapter. 
        (Or just 
        |> span
            class=code-cartouche
            theta0 = atan2(B, A)
        \ if we were writing python!)

        !! Let $(R_0, \theta_0)$ be the polar coordinates of
        !! $(A, B)$. Then

        !! $$
        !! \begin{aligned}
        !! A &=         R_0\cos\theta_0,\\
        !! B &= \up{1.5}R_0\rt{0.1}\sin\theta_0\,
        !! \end{aligned}
        !! $$

        !! by definition of polar coordinates, from which

        !! $$
        !! \begin{aligned}
        !! A\cos x + B\sin x \,=\,\,&\up{1.5}  (R_0\cos\theta_0)\cos x + (R_0\sin\theta_0)\sin x\\
        !! =\,\,&\up{1.5}  R_0(\cos x\cos\theta_0 + \sin x \sin \theta_0)\\
        !! =\,\,&\up{1.5}  R_0\cos(x - \theta_0)
        !! \end{aligned}
        !! $$

        !! where the “angle-difference formula”
        !! !! where, in the last equality, we use the “angle-difference formula” (cf. Exercise >>_unit_dot_product_geometric_interpretation_)

        !! |> Boxed
        !!     $$
        !!     \cos(u - v) = \cos u\cos v + \sin u\sin v
        !!     $$

        !! (cf. Exercise >>_unit_dot_product_geometric_interpretation_)
        !! is used in the last equality.
